WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
I told the agent which village, and

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:03.870
it gave me today’s weather.

3
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.100
I had asked about tomorrow,

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.440
three messages earlier!

5
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.880
It had simply forgotten.

6
00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.190
Where did my words go?

7
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.370
Today: an agent’s memory,

8
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.150
and why it forgets.

9
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.640
A model has no memory of

10
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.300
its own between turns.

11
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.610
Each time, the app hands it the text so

12
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.750
far, and it reads it all again.

13
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.320
The most it can read at once is

14
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.880
called its context window.

15
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.510
Darsi Tiny’s window holds three

16
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.230
hundred and eighty-four tokens,

17
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.130
about three or four requests.

18
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.240
Big cloud models read about one

19
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.780
hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

20
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.230
Bigger windows forget less, but every

21
00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.250
request costs more time and money.

22
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.200
When the text is longer than the

23
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.940
window, something has to go.

24
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.990
Whatever falls out, the

25
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.430
model never sees again.

26
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.640
It isn’t hidden somewhere: for

27
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.180
the model, it’s gone.

28
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.420
The app decides what stays.

29
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.120
Keep everything, and the window fills:

30
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.240
the fourth answer is cut off.

31
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.900
Drop the oldest turns, and the

32
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.480
start of a task can vanish.

33
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.720
Or keep short notes: what was

34
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.360
asked, and what was answered.

35
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.080
Quick check!

36
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.120
The app drops the oldest turns.

37
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.270
I said yes to the agent’s

38
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.690
question, and it just greeted me.

39
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:29.790
Why?

40
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.780
The question had fallen out.

41
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.360
Without it, a lone yes means nothing, so

42
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.560
Darsi Tiny treated it as a greeting.

43
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.350
That’s the risk of dropping the oldest

44
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.230
turns: the start of a task goes first.

45
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.410
Notes are the smart middle way.

46
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.080
A finished request keeps only the

47
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.660
question and the answer; its tool

48
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.380
calls and results go.

49
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.370
In the lab, with notes, all seven

50
00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.170
messages work in the same window.

51
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.720
Memory is also a privacy question.

52
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.930
Whatever stays in the window, or in

53
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.210
saved notes, the model can repeat.

54
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.220
So keep pupils’ names and marks out, and

55
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.420
start a new conversation for each task.

56
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.160
In the app, send the messages with Keep

57
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.480
everything, and watch the window fill.

58
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.490
Then try Drop the oldest at one hundred

59
00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.830
and twenty-eight tokens, and say yes.

60
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.060
Last, keep notes, and see all seven work.

61
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.090
Your turn.

62
00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.770
A colleague pastes thirty pupils’

63
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.330
reports into one chat with a small

64
00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.350
agent, then asks about the first pupil.

65
00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.250
What might go wrong?

66
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.090
The first reports may have fallen out,

67
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.070
so the answer could be made up.

68
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.880
Remember: the window is the

69
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.260
model’s only memory.

70
00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.950
What falls out is gone.

71
00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.210
And the app chooses what stays.

72
00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:00.970
Try it in class tomorrow:

73
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.810
play the ten-word window.

74
00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.230
Tell a story on the board, one word each,

75
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.290
but rub out all but the last ten words.

76
00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.490
Then ask about the beginning:

77
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.210
who still remembers it?
